//feedback
Not quite — your pipe is doing the transformation immediately, but the task wants pipe to be a function creator that accepts functions and returns a new function.

//what to takeway
Step 1: calling pipe
  - pipe(trimString, toUpperCase, addGreeting)

So inside pipe:
fns = [trimString, toUpperCase, addGreeting]

It returns a new function:
(value) => fns.reduce((acc, fn) => fn(acc), value
So now:
transform = returned function     
                      
fns.reduce((acc, fn) => fn(acc), value)
Which means:
[trimString, toUpperCase, addGreeting].reduce((acc, fn) => fn(acc), "  john  ")

Step 2: calling transform("  john  ")
Now:
value = "  john  "
We evaluate:

fns.reduce((acc, fn) => fn(acc), value)
Which means:

[trimString, toUpperCase, addGreeting].reduce((acc, fn) => fn(acc), "  john  ")

How reduce works here
Yes — here it is in a compact table:

Step	  || acc before	|| fn used	    || Function call	    || Result	    || acc after

Initial     	—	            —	            —	                      —            " john  "

1	          "  john  "	  trimString	    trimString("  john  ")	"john"	      "john"
2	          "john"	      toUpperCase	    toUpperCase("john")	    "JOHN"	      "JOHN"
3	          "JOHN"	      addGreeting	    addGreeting("JOHN")	  "Hello JOHN"	"Hello JOHN"



